          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a document refers to the J45 family, is that a code or a category?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: J45 is a category, not a billable code.** The asthma coding guidance describes J45 as the category that holds the asthma codes, with severity and exacerbation axes. A general rule in the ICD-10-CM coding guidance says most categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is billable. So a document that cites only \"J45\" is pointing to the category, not to a complete code.\n\nI could not confirm from the retrieved material the exact range J45.2 through J45.9, or which of those codes are billable. The guidance says the category contains more specific codes and that some variants, such as exercise-induced bronchospasm and cough variant asthma, have their own codes. For the exact list, check the current ICD-10-CM tables from the National Center for Health Statistics or CMS, since codes can change between years.\n\n**How \"family\" is usually read:** \"Family\" is informal. It is not a formal ICD-10 term. Coding guidance uses it loosely for a group of related categories or codes, such as the \"hypertensive disease family\" or diabetes. When a document says \"the J45 family,\" it most likely means J45 and its more specific asthma codes together, not a single code.\n\nSource: \"Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes\" and \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\" (both from the US ICD-10-CM educational summary), plus \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
